# CSS Selectors: Grouping, Descendants & Children

## 1. Grouping selectors

Use commas to apply the same CSS declarations to multiple selectors.

```css
h1, h2, .title, #subtitle {
  color: black;
}
```

- `h1` — element selector
- `h2` — element selector
- `.title` — class selector
- `#subtitle` — ID selector
- `,` — groups selectors so they share the declarations

## 2. Descendant selector

A space selects matching elements anywhere inside another element, at any nesting depth.

```css
.parent p {
  color: black;
}
```

This selects all `<p>` elements inside `.parent`, including nested paragraphs.

## 3. Child combinator (`>`)

The `>` combinator selects matching elements that are **direct children** of another element.

```css
.parent > p {
  color: black;
}
```

This selects only `<p>` elements directly inside `.parent`, not paragraphs nested inside another element.

## Key difference

```css
.parent p {
  color: black;
}
```

Selects all descendant paragraphs.

```css
.parent > p {
  color: black;
}
```

Selects only direct child paragraphs.

## What I practiced

- Grouping multiple selectors with commas
- Combining element, class, and ID selectors
- Selecting descendants using a space
- Selecting direct children using `>`
- Applying shared styles without repeating declarations
